The 12-hour period shows a classic day-night curve. Evening (minutes 1–149, 21:00–23:29) was the busiest phase: steady mixed traffic of 8–25 two-wheelers per minute, frequent autos, cars and a constant rotation of buses and goods vehicles, with repeated signal-cycled queuing and recurring vehicles stopped on the zebra. Late night (minutes 150–199, 23:29–00:44) tapered to light post-midnight traffic on a wet road, with the first rider-fall incident (min 157) and ambulance sightings. Overnight (minutes 200–499, ~00:45–05:19) was the quietest stretch: greyscale IR footage, traffic reduced to a trickle of two-wheelers and isolated cars/trucks, pedestrians almost entirely absent, and several ambiguous events (possible fallen riders at 213 and 384, debris at 263/390, animals at 357/386/429). Pre-dawn into early morning (minutes 500–574, ~05:19–06:34) saw a gradual build-up, with goods vehicles (TATA tippers), refuse trucks and the first named-brand reads. Morning (minutes 575–719, 06:34–08:59) restored steady light-to-moderate flow dominated by green-and-yellow auto-rickshaws and two-wheelers, with city buses, handcarts, cyclists, hi-vis workers and regular pedestrian crossings — but notably, traffic continued filtering through on an amber/red signal (mins 589, 600–624). Recurring themes across the whole period: heavy glare/pixelation degrading all identification; wet/reflective road surface; vehicles and buses stopping on or over the zebra crossing; stationary buses blocking the camera; helmetless riders; and stray dogs on the carriageway.

