## 8. PATTERN OF ACTIVITY

The footage follows a clear 24-hour rhythm at a fixed transport-node forecourt. In early daylight (minutes 0–80) the scene is busy and functional: buses, autos and commuters passing, small waiting groups, shuttered shops awaiting opening. From minute 81 the scene turns to pre-dawn darkness and the dominant pattern becomes pavement sleeping — groups of 3–10 people settling against the closed shutter, with personal effects (white sneakers, blankets, bags, phone-lit screens) arranged beside them, while road traffic thins to sparse buses, autos and cars with headlight glare. Through the night hours the same small casts persist for 25-minute stretches at a time: a checkered-shirt man on a white scooter (minutes 181–200), a yellow-shirt man eating at the kerb (minutes 366–414), a white-shirt man on his phone (minutes 175–199, 425–449), and a pair huddled over a phone at the planter (minutes 575–598). The white hatchback at the left kerb recurs as a night fixture with doors opening intermittently and various people attending it (minutes 210, 216, 275–299, 444–499, 467). Early morning (minutes 100–180, and again ~445 onward) brings commuter surges — loitering groups of young men with backpacks, police patrols in khaki (minutes 105, 109, 117, 146, 149, 151), a saree-clad woman working at a shutter (minutes 107–114), and heavy GSRTC bus movement. Saturday-night footage (minutes 550–699) shows steady bus turnover at the exit lane and the parking bay growing from 4–6 to 10–15 two-wheelers. Constant across all 718 minutes: closed shutters, the stationary parked two-wheeler bay (several bikes never move at any point), green-and-yellow auto-rickshaws as the dominant road traffic, abandoned sandals on the pavement, a wet/reflective road in most night segments, GSRTC as the overwhelming bus operator, and severe timestamp discontinuities making the true elapsed chronology unreliable.

